TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Inclination of 87.79° is prograde and cannot be sun-synchronous; it also contradicts the plotted ~97.8° at 600 km and the claimed retrograde orbit."},{"where":"Orbit (COE table)","issue":"Semi-major axis 7171 km is wrong; 6371 km + 600 km = 6971 km."},{"where":"Orbit (COE table)","issue":"RAAN 'initial value indicates the location of the launch facility' is incorrect; RAAN is set by the epoch and sun-synchronous condition, not the launch site."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Claims the true anomaly is undefined for a circular orbit. It is defined from the ascending node, with argument of latitude as the usual replacement for ω+ν."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"States Kourou is at 45.05°N and 'very close to the equator'. Kourou is ~5.2°N; the latitude is wrong and the justification is inconsistent."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Proposes Vega E as the launch vehicle while acknowledging it is unbuilt, and the executive summary says 'direct launch' despite a piggyback plan."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mean contact duration of 580.8 s is ~9.7 min, not 'almost twenty minutes'. The access table also repeats access number 13 and lists only 14 unique passes."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Claims a near-polar ground station maximises passes, but Mt Pleasant (~43°S) is not polar; pass frequency and number of stations are not quantified."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Uncontrolled re-entry from 600 km is not checked against decay time or the 25-year guideline. The 'graveyard' manoeuvre requires propulsion, which contradicts the 'without thrusters' statement."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Drag-sail sentence is truncated and unfinished."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Wikipedia is cited despite being explicitly unacceptable. Reference [10] is used for two unrelated claims, the entry is malformed, and citations [11]–[74] have no bibliography entries."}],"overall":"Good STK-based effort with relevant screenshots and access data, but core orbital calculations contain errors, including a prograde inclination for a claimed sun-synchronous orbit and an incorrect semi-major axis. EOL is incomplete and the reference list is inconsistent and includes Wikipedia. Requirements are mostly addressed but several need more rigour.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Primary mission, rationale and single-spacecraft choice are well argued with references. Users are only implied (marine and conservation scientists); operator and customers are not explicitly identified. The executive summary's 'direct launch' conflicts with the later piggyback approach. Overall detailed but not fully complete.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"a direct launch into a retrograde sun-synchronous polar orbit"},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"is operated and accessed from Australia ground facilities"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":24.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Substantial STK evidence and access tables support the design. However, the 87.79° inclination is prograde, contradicting the claimed retrograde sun-synchronous orbit and the plotted ~97.8°. The semi-major axis is arithmetically wrong (6371+600 is 6971 km). Kourou latitude is misstated, the contact-duration claim conflicts with the table, and the EOL analysis is incomplete with no decay or delta-V assessment.","evidence":[{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of"},{"where":"Orbit (COE table)","quote":"Earth radius of 6371km plus a proposed altitude of 600km as previously explained."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost twenty minutes was achieved"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"utilising a large surface area to"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarises the main segments and identifies the Vega E launch risk with a fallback. It is largely a restatement and does not draw evidence-based conclusions on performance or EOL. It also overstates the mission outcome as preventing bleaching.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A specific launch vehicle – the Vega E – has been selected although it has not been built yet."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"The Mt Pleasant Radio Observatory is well-located to receive data from this orbit type constantly and for long durations."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear headings and a contents page give reasonable structure. Presentation is undermined by a truncated sentence, a duplicated access number, a broken reference entry, and a duplicated [10] citation. Citations up to [74] have no list entries, and Wikipedia is cited. Typos and some unlabelled or uncaptioned tables reduce clarity.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"utilising a large surface area to"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[10 d."},{"where":"Launch","quote":"The Block 5 variation is currently the only active Falcon 9 variant"}]}]}
